{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for tenant criminal convictions (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1)","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault or threats against the landlord, theft on the premises, drug activity, or human trafficking; a three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3 days' notice; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the state Eviction Law; the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside at the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"In landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, a tenant may be removed only by court order and only for the listed causes (holdover, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, rule violations); three months' notice applies to at-will or year-to-year tenancies and one month to month-to-month tenancies.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1-month notice (month-to-month)","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give at least three working days' notice to the County Welfare Board before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (owner occupancy and demolition)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months; a landlord who evicts to board up, demolish or permanently retire a building from residential use may not use the property residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential use bar","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtain eviction based on personal occupancy of the unit or permanent removal of the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages plus attorney fees and costs; a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation; property cannot be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal protections for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, substantial lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, makes a good faith complaint to a government authority, joins a tenant organization, or refuses an adverse lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All rental dwellings except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections after foreclosure (90-day notice)","requirement":"Before evicting a tenant because of foreclosure, a landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit when the buyer wants to occupy the property as a primary residence; a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days' notice of sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law providing longer time periods or additional tenant protections in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled veterans who lived in the building at least one year before conversion may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within limits; applications are generally filed within 60 days.","key_value":"40 years' protection; 60-day application period","coverage_conditions":"Tenants aged 62 or older, permanently disabled, or disabled veterans (60% or more service-connected) who lived in a building being converted at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County)","requirement":"Extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; Hudson County is currently the only qualified county.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; at present only Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County only"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
